{"answer":"No. The argument contradicts its own lowest-terms hypothesis: if a and b are both even, then 2 is a common divisor. With the standard well-ordering principle used explicitly to obtain a lowest-denominator representation and to establish parity, the reductio proves that sqrt(2) is irrational, not rational.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume sqrt(2) is rational, choose a representation with least positive denominator, and use parity to derive a common factor of 2.","id":"parity-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The least-denominator construction supplies the previously missing lowest-terms representation, and the contradiction discharges the rationality assumption."},{"description":"Treat the conclusion that both numerator and denominator are even as evidence for rationality.","id":"claimed-direction","outcome":"rejected","reason":"Both being even contradicts lowest terms."}],"change_log":["Replaced the unsupported lowest-terms setup with a temporary assumption that sqrt(2) is rational and a least-positive-denominator construction.","Made the parity dichotomy explicit as an application of the named well-ordering principle.","Added the proof steps establishing that the least-denominator representation has no common divisor greater than 1.","The reductio now discharges the temporary rationality assumption, rather than merely one arbitrarily assumed representation.","No source citations or tool receipts were supplied; none are asserted.","Added rationality-assumption as a dependency of irrationality-conclusion so its discharge at lowest-terms-conflict is structurally represented by a dependent deduction claim whose proof steps include that discharge step."],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"Question premise: \"A proof claims that sqrt(2) is rational: write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms, square to get a^2=2b^2, and conclude that a and b are both even.\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"displayed-argument","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The displayed argument starts from a lowest-terms representation and derives that both numerator and denominator are even.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Definition of square root.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"root-definition","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"sqrt(2) denotes the positive number whose square is 2.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Definitions of rationality, evenness, and divisibility.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"integer-definitions","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"A number is rational if it equals m/n for integers m,n with n nonzero; an integer is even if it equals 2k for an integer k; and g divides n if n=gt for an integer t.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Well-ordering principle for positive integers: every nonempty set of positive integers has a least member; applied in steps least-denominator and parity-decomposition.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"well-ordering-principle","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Every nonempty set of positive integers has a least element.","step_ids":["least-denominator","parity-decomposition"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the well-ordering principle in step parity-decomposition.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["well-ordering-principle","integer-definitions"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"parity-dichotomy","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every integer n, either n is even or n=2k+1 for some integer k.","step_ids":["parity-decomposition"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary negation of the intended conclusion that sqrt(2) is irrational.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"id":"rationality-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["rational-representation","positive-denominator","least-denominator","lowest-terms-property","square-equation","a-square-even","a-even","substitute-a","b-square-even","b-even","lowest-terms-conflict"],"statement":"Assume temporarily that sqrt(2) is rational.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps rational-representation through lowest-terms-property from the local rationality assumption and the well-ordering principle.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["rationality-assumption","integer-definitions","well-ordering-principle"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-representation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, there are integers a,b with b positive, sqrt(2)=a/b, and no common divisor of a and b greater than 1.","step_ids":["rational-representation","positive-denominator","least-denominator","lowest-terms-property"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the least-denominator representation in step square-equation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-representation","root-definition"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"squared-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a^2=2b^2.","step_ids":["square-equation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps a-square-even and a-even using the parity dichotomy.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["squared-equation","parity-dichotomy","integer-definitions"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-is-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a is even.","step_ids":["a-square-even","a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps substitute-a through b-even using the parity dichotomy.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["squared-equation","a-is-even","parity-dichotomy","integer-definitions"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-is-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b is even.","step_ids":["substitute-a","b-square-even","b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step lowest-terms-conflict from the least-denominator property and the evenness of a and b.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-representation","a-is-even","b-is-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary rationality assumption yields a contradiction.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Reductio in step conclude-irrational, after discharge of the local rationality assumption at lowest-terms-conflict.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["rationality-assumption","lowest-terms-contradiction"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"irrationality-conclusion","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"sqrt(2) is irrational.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict","conclude-irrational"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"parity-decomposition","justification":"By the division algorithm with divisor 2 (proved from the well-ordering principle), n=2q+r for integers q,r with r equal to 0 or 1. Thus n=2q or n=2q+1.","statement":"Establish the parity dichotomy for every integer n."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"rational-representation","justification":"By the temporary rationality assumption and the definition of rationality, choose integers m,n with n nonzero and sqrt(2)=m/n.","statement":"Choose an integer representation sqrt(2)=m/n with n nonzero."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["rational-representation"],"id":"positive-denominator","justification":"If n is negative, replace m,n by -m,-n; otherwise retain them. This gives a representation with positive denominator.","statement":"Obtain a representation of sqrt(2) having a positive integer denominator."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["positive-denominator"],"id":"least-denominator","justification":"The set of positive denominators occurring in integer representations of sqrt(2) is nonempty by step positive-denominator. Apply the well-ordering principle and call its least element b; choose its associated numerator a.","statement":"Choose integers a,b with b the least positive denominator among all integer representations of sqrt(2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["least-denominator"],"id":"lowest-terms-property","justification":"If an integer g>1 divided both a and b, write a=gc and b=gd. Since b is positive, d is positive and d<b; moreover sqrt(2)=c/d. This contradicts the minimality of b. Hence no such g exists.","statement":"The selected integers a,b have no common divisor greater than 1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["least-denominator"],"id":"square-equation","justification":"Square sqrt(2)=a/b, use (sqrt(2))^2=2, and multiply by the nonzero number b^2.","statement":"Derive a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation"],"id":"a-square-even","justification":"The equation a^2=2b^2 writes a^2 as twice an integer.","statement":"a^2 is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["a-square-even","parity-decomposition"],"id":"a-even","justification":"By parity decomposition, a is even or a=2k+1. In the latter case a^2=4k^2+4k+1=2(2k^2+2k)+1, which is not even, contradicting step a-square-even.","statement":"There is an integer c such that a=2c."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation","a-even"],"id":"substitute-a","justification":"Substitute a=2c into a^2=2b^2 to get 4c^2=2b^2, then divide by 2.","statement":"Derive b^2=2c^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["substitute-a"],"id":"b-square-even","justification":"The equation b^2=2c^2 writes b^2 as twice an integer.","statement":"b^2 is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["b-square-even","parity-decomposition"],"id":"b-even","justification":"By parity decomposition, b is even or b=2k+1. The latter makes b^2=2(2k^2+2k)+1, contradicting step b-square-even.","statement":"There is an integer d such that b=2d."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-property","a-even","b-even"],"id":"lowest-terms-conflict","justification":"Steps a-even and b-even show that 2 divides both a and b, contrary to step lowest-terms-property. This contradiction discharges the temporary rationality assumption.","statement":"Obtain a contradiction."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"id":"conclude-irrational","justification":"The assumption that sqrt(2) is rational has been discharged by contradiction; therefore sqrt(2) is not rational.","statement":"Conclude that sqrt(2) is irrational."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
